CALIFORNIA DEALER LOGISTICS SOLUTIONS
INDEPENDENT HAULER PARTNERSHIP AGREEMENT

THIS PARTNERSHIP AGREEMENT ("Agreement") is entered into as of _________________ ("Effective Date") by and between:

CALIFORNIA DEALER LOGISTICS SOLUTIONS, LLC, a California limited liability company ("CDLS"), with its principal place of business at Sacramento, California,

AND

_________________________________ ("Partner Hauler"), with its principal place of business at _________________________________

RECITALS

WHEREAS, CDLS operates a zero-emission vehicle (ZEV) car hauling network serving California automotive dealerships and is positioned to address the Advanced Clean Fleet (ACF) regulation compliance challenges facing the automotive logistics industry;

WHEREAS, Partner Hauler is an established automotive hauling operator with existing dealer relationships, operational expertise, and licensed professional drivers;

WHEREAS, traditional haulers face capital expenditure requirements of approximately $185,000 per truck to transition to ZEV compliance;

WHEREAS, CDLS seeks to expand its dealer network and operational capacity through partnerships with experienced haulers who bring established relationships and market knowledge;

WHEREAS, Partner Hauler seeks to maintain and grow its automotive hauling business while achieving ACF compliance without prohibitive capital investment;

NOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements contained herein, the parties agree as follows:

ARTICLE 1: PARTNERSHIP STRUCTURE

1.1 Independent Contractor Relationship
Partner Hauler shall operate as an independent contractor, not as an employee, agent, or joint venturer of CDLS. Partner Hauler retains control over the means and methods of providing hauling services, subject to compliance with this Agreement and applicable regulations.

1.2 Partnership Tier
Partner Hauler shall be designated in one of the following tiers based on commitment level and volume capacity:

□ TIER 1 - FOUNDATIONAL PARTNER: 1-3 trucks, minimum 40 loads/month
□ TIER 2 - GROWTH PARTNER: 4-7 trucks, minimum 100 loads/month  
□ TIER 3 - STRATEGIC PARTNER: 8+ trucks, minimum 200 loads/month

1.3 Partnership Territory
Partner Hauler's primary service territory shall be: _________________________________
Additional territories may be added by mutual written agreement.

ARTICLE 2: CDLS OBLIGATIONS AND FLEET ACCESS

2.1 Zero-Emission Vehicle Fleet
CDLS shall provide Partner Hauler with access to ZEV car hauler trucks meeting the following specifications:
   a) Vehicles compliant with California ACF regulations
   b) Properly maintained and inspected equipment
   c) Appropriate insurance coverage as specified in Article 7
   d) GPS tracking and dispatch integration

2.2 Fleet Allocation
   a) Tier 1 Partners: Access to 1-3 dedicated ZEV trucks
   b) Tier 2 Partners: Access to 4-7 dedicated ZEV trucks, priority scheduling
   c) Tier 3 Partners: Access to 8+ ZEV trucks, priority scheduling, fleet expansion planning

2.3 Technology and Systems
CDLS shall provide Partner Hauler with:
   a) Access to CDLS proprietary inventory management software
   b) Dispatch and routing optimization tools
   c) Real-time load tracking capabilities
   d) Electronic logging device (ELD) compliance systems
   e) Customer relationship management (CRM) integration

2.4 Operational Support
CDLS shall provide:
   a) Dispatch coordination and load optimization
   b) Insurance and risk management
   c) ACF compliance documentation and reporting
   d) Maintenance scheduling and coordination
   e) Customer invoicing and collections

2.5 Marketing and Business Development
CDLS shall:
   a) Market ZEV hauling services to target dealerships
   b) Leverage Partner Hauler's dealer relationships in business development
   c) Provide co-branded marketing materials
   d) Support Partner Hauler in dealer relationship management

ARTICLE 3: PARTNER HAULER OBLIGATIONS

3.1 Driver Provision
Partner Hauler shall provide:
   a) Licensed, qualified, and properly trained drivers
   b) Compliance with all FMCSA and California DMV regulations
   c) Driver drug and alcohol testing program compliance
   d) Ongoing driver training and safety programs

3.2 Operational Excellence
Partner Hauler shall:
   a) Maintain on-time delivery performance of 95% or higher
   b) Achieve damage-free delivery rate of 98% or higher
   c) Respond to dispatch assignments within agreed timeframes
   d) Maintain professional relationships with dealer clients
   e) Comply with all applicable transportation regulations

3.3 Dealer Relationship Development
Partner Hauler shall:
   a) Introduce CDLS to existing dealer relationships
   b) Facilitate transition of servicing agreements to CDLS
   c) Support dealer onboarding to CDLS platform
   d) Provide market intelligence on dealer needs and competitive landscape

3.4 Volume Commitments
Partner Hauler commits to minimum monthly volumes as specified in Section 1.2, averaged over each calendar quarter. Failure to meet 80% of minimum volume for two consecutive quarters shall trigger review and potential tier adjustment.

3.5 Technology Adoption
Partner Hauler shall:
   a) Utilize CDLS dispatch and inventory management systems
   b) Provide real-time load status updates
   c) Complete required system training
   d) Maintain data security and confidentiality

ARTICLE 4: COMPENSATION AND REVENUE SHARING

4.1 Revenue Share Model
Gross revenue from loads completed by Partner Hauler shall be distributed as follows:

TIER 1 - FOUNDATIONAL PARTNER:
   • Partner Hauler: 60% of gross load revenue
   • CDLS: 40% of gross load revenue

TIER 2 - GROWTH PARTNER:
   • Partner Hauler: 65% of gross load revenue
   • CDLS: 35% of gross load revenue

TIER 3 - STRATEGIC PARTNER:
   • Partner Hauler: 70% of gross load revenue
   • CDLS: 30% of gross load revenue

4.2 Payment Terms
CDLS shall remit Partner Hauler's revenue share within 15 days following month-end, accompanied by detailed load reporting.

4.3 Dealer Relationship Transition Bonuses
Upon successful transition of a dealer relationship to CDLS (defined as execution of service agreement and completion of 50+ loads), Partner Hauler shall receive:
   • Small Dealer (0-100 units/month): $2,500 transition bonus
   • Medium Dealer (101-300 units/month): $7,500 transition bonus
   • Large Dealer (301+ units/month): $15,000 transition bonus

4.4 Performance Incentives
Additional quarterly bonuses based on:
   a) Volume performance exceeding tier minimums: 2% bonus on excess volume
   b) Safety performance (zero accidents): $1,000 per quarter
   c) Customer satisfaction scores >4.5/5.0: $500 per quarter
   d) On-time performance >98%: $500 per quarter

4.5 Fuel and Operating Costs
Fuel, charging, and routine operating costs for CDLS fleet vehicles shall be borne by CDLS and deducted prior to revenue sharing calculation.

ARTICLE 5: TERM AND TERMINATION

5.1 Initial Term
This Agreement shall commence on the Effective Date and continue for an initial term of two (2) years.

5.2 Renewal
This Agreement shall automatically renew for successive one (1) year terms unless either party provides written notice of non-renewal at least ninety (90) days prior to the end of the then-current term.

5.3 Termination for Cause
Either party may terminate this Agreement for cause upon thirty (30) days written notice if:
   a) Material breach of Agreement terms uncured after notice period
   b) Bankruptcy or insolvency proceedings
   c) Loss of required licenses or regulatory authority
   d) Failure to maintain required insurance
   e) Safety or compliance violations threatening business operations

5.4 Termination Without Cause
Either party may terminate this Agreement without cause upon one hundred eighty (180) days written notice.

5.5 Transition Upon Termination
   a) Partner Hauler shall complete all loads in progress
   b) CDLS shall remit all earned compensation within thirty (30) days
   c) Dealer relationship assignments shall be governed by Article 6
   d) Partner Hauler shall return all CDLS property and equipment
   e) Technology access shall be terminated within forty-eight (48) hours

ARTICLE 6: DEALER RELATIONSHIPS AND ASSIGNMENT

6.1 Relationship Assignment
Dealer relationships introduced by Partner Hauler and transitioned to CDLS service agreements shall be deemed CDLS customer relationships, with Partner Hauler retaining preferred provider status for servicing such accounts during the term of this Agreement.

6.2 Right of First Refusal
For dealer relationships originated by Partner Hauler, Partner Hauler shall have right of first refusal to service loads from such dealers, subject to capacity and performance standards.

6.3 Post-Termination Treatment
Upon termination of this Agreement:
   a) If terminated by Partner Hauler without cause: Dealer relationships remain with CDLS
   b) If terminated by CDLS without cause: Partner Hauler may solicit assigned dealers after 12-month waiting period
   c) If terminated for cause by CDLS: All dealer relationships remain with CDLS permanently
   d) If terminated for cause by Partner Hauler: Partner Hauler may immediately resume servicing originated dealers

6.4 Transition Compensation
If Partner Hauler exits partnership and dealer relationships remain with CDLS, Partner Hauler shall receive ongoing referral fees of 3% of gross revenue from originated dealers for twenty-four (24) months following termination.

ARTICLE 7: INSURANCE AND LIABILITY

7.1 CDLS Insurance Obligations
CDLS shall maintain:
   a) Commercial Auto Liability: $2,000,000 combined single limit
   b) Cargo Insurance: $250,000 per load
   c) General Liability: $2,000,000 per occurrence
   d) Garage Liability: $1,000,000 per occurrence

7.2 Partner Hauler Insurance Obligations
Partner Hauler shall maintain:
   a) Workers' Compensation as required by California law
   b) Employer's Liability: $1,000,000
   c) Commercial General Liability: $1,000,000 per occurrence

7.3 Additional Insured
Each party shall name the other as additional insured on relevant policies.

7.4 Certificates of Insurance
Each party shall provide certificates of insurance evidencing required coverage upon execution and renewal.

7.5 Liability Allocation
   a) Vehicle/Equipment Liability: CDLS responsible for losses arising from vehicle defects or maintenance failures
   b) Driver Operations Liability: Partner Hauler responsible for losses arising from driver negligence or operational errors
   c) Shared Liability: Allocated proportionally based on fault determination

ARTICLE 8: COMPLIANCE AND REGULATORY MATTERS

8.1 ACF Compliance
CDLS warrants that all fleet vehicles provided meet California Advanced Clean Fleet regulation requirements. CDLS shall maintain all necessary ACF compliance documentation and reporting.

8.2 Operating Authority
   a) CDLS maintains primary operating authority for all loads
   b) Partner Hauler maintains valid motor carrier authority and DOT number
   c) Each party responsible for maintaining respective licenses and permits

8.3 Safety and Compliance Programs
   a) Partner Hauler shall maintain FMCSA safety rating of Satisfactory or better
   b) Partner Hauler responsible for driver hours of service compliance
   c) CDLS shall provide safety training resources and support

8.4 Reporting and Documentation
Partner Hauler shall provide CDLS with:
   a) Quarterly safety performance reports
   b) Driver qualification files upon request
   c) Accident and incident reports within 24 hours
   d) Compliance inspection results

ARTICLE 9: INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY AND CONFIDENTIALITY

9.1 CDLS Intellectual Property
All CDLS technology, software, processes, and business methods remain the exclusive property of CDLS. Partner Hauler receives limited license to use such property solely for partnership purposes during the Agreement term.

9.2 Confidential Information
Each party shall maintain confidentiality of the other party's:
   a) Customer lists and pricing information
   b) Proprietary technology and processes
   c) Financial information and business strategies
   d) Trade secrets and competitive information

9.3 Data Ownership
Load data, dealer information, and operational data generated through the partnership shall be jointly owned, with each party retaining perpetual access to data related to their contributed relationships.

9.4 Non-Disclosure Survival
Confidentiality obligations shall survive termination of this Agreement for three (3) years.

ARTICLE 10: NON-COMPETE AND NON-SOLICITATION

10.1 Non-Compete During Term
During the term of this Agreement, Partner Hauler shall not provide automotive hauling services in California using diesel or internal combustion engine vehicles that compete with CDLS's ZEV service offering.

10.2 Dealer Non-Solicitation
For dealer relationships transitioned to CDLS during the partnership, Partner Hauler agrees not to solicit such dealers for competing services during the Agreement term and for twelve (12) months following termination, except as provided in Section 6.3.

10.3 Employee Non-Solicitation
Neither party shall solicit or hire the other party's employees during the Agreement term and for twelve (12) months following termination without written consent.

10.4 Reasonableness
The parties acknowledge these restrictions are reasonable and necessary to protect legitimate business interests.

ARTICLE 11: PILOT PROGRAM AND PERFORMANCE REVIEW

11.1 Initial Pilot Period
The first ninety (90) days shall constitute a pilot program during which:
   a) Either party may terminate upon thirty (30) days notice
   b) Performance metrics shall be established and tracked
   c) Dealer relationships remain non-exclusive
   d) Compensation may be adjusted by mutual agreement

11.2 Quarterly Business Reviews
Parties shall conduct quarterly business review meetings to assess:
   a) Volume and revenue performance
   b) Safety and compliance metrics
   c) Dealer relationship development progress
   d) Technology adoption and utilization
   e) Strategic growth opportunities

11.3 Annual Strategic Planning
Parties shall conduct annual strategic planning sessions to:
   a) Review tier status and volume commitments
   b) Discuss fleet expansion needs
   c) Evaluate market opportunities
   d) Adjust compensation structures if warranted

ARTICLE 12: REPRESENTATIONS AND WARRANTIES

12.1 CDLS Representations
CDLS represents and warrants:
   a) It is duly organized and validly existing under California law
   b) It has full authority to enter this Agreement
   c) It maintains all required operating authorities and licenses
   d) Its fleet vehicles meet all ACF requirements
   e) It maintains required insurance coverage

12.2 Partner Hauler Representations
Partner Hauler represents and warrants:
   a) It is duly organized and validly existing
   b) It has full authority to enter this Agreement
   c) It maintains valid motor carrier operating authority
   d) Its drivers are properly licensed and qualified
   e) It maintains required insurance coverage
   f) It is not subject to any non-compete or similar restriction that would prevent performance

ARTICLE 13: INDEMNIFICATION

13.1 Mutual Indemnification
Each party shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless the other party from claims arising from:
   a) Breach of representations and warranties
   b) Gross negligence or willful misconduct
   c) Violation of applicable laws
   d) Third-party claims arising from party's operations

13.2 Indemnification Procedures
Indemnified party shall provide prompt notice of claims and cooperate in defense. Indemnifying party shall control defense and settlement.

ARTICLE 14: DISPUTE RESOLUTION

14.1 Negotiation
Disputes shall first be addressed through good-faith negotiation between senior executives of each party.

14.2 Mediation
If negotiation fails within thirty (30) days, parties shall submit to mediation before a mutually agreed mediator in Sacramento, California.

14.3 Arbitration
If mediation fails, disputes shall be resolved through binding arbitration in Sacramento, California under JAMS rules, with one arbitrator mutually selected by the parties.

14.4 Governing Law
This Agreement shall be governed by California law, excluding conflict of laws principles.

ARTICLE 15: GENERAL PROVISIONS

15.1 Entire Agreement
This Agreement constitutes the entire agreement between parties and supersedes all prior negotiations and understandings.

15.2 Amendments
Amendments must be in writing signed by both parties.

15.3 Assignment
Neither party may assign this Agreement without written consent, except CDLS may assign to a successor entity in connection with a merger or sale.

15.4 Force Majeure
Neither party liable for delays caused by circumstances beyond reasonable control.

15.5 Notices
All notices shall be in writing and delivered to addresses specified below:

CDLS:
California Dealer Logistics Solutions, LLC
[Address]
Attention: [Name]
Email: [Email]

Partner Hauler:
[Name]
[Address]
Attention: [Name]
Email: [Email]

15.6 Severability
If any provision is held invalid, remaining provisions continue in full force.

15.7 Waiver
Failure to enforce any right does not waive that right.

15.8 Counterparts
This Agreement may be executed in counterparts, each constituting an original.

15.9 Survival
Articles 6 (Dealer Relationships), 9 (Confidentiality), 10 (Non-Compete), 13 (Indemnification), and 14 (Dispute Resolution) shall survive termination.

IN WITNESS WHEREOF, the parties have executed this Agreement as of the Effective Date.

